<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<RecordType xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>. RecordType.Description is capped
        at 255 characters. A comment is repo-only and never reaches the Metadata API. It must
        sit INSIDE the root element - a comment above <RecordType> breaks `sf` deploy at
        source conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent xml file".

        THE ACQUISITIONS BROKER LEAD. Every Lead that existed before 2026-08-10 is this type,
        and every Lead the inbound email pipeline, the Broker Portal, or a manual acquisitions
        entry creates is this type.

        THIS RECORD TYPE IS LOAD-BEARING FOR SECURITY, NOT PRESENTATION. Once Lead OWD is
        Private (plan Task 12), the criteria-based sharing rule Lead_Acquisition_Broker is the
        ONLY thing granting DPEG_Acquisitions_Team access to these rows. A Lead left without a
        record type matches NO sharing rule and becomes invisible to everyone except its owner.
        That is why the backfill (Task 3) is gated on a zero-row query and why the Apex stamps
        (Tasks 4 and 5) ship BEFORE the backfill runs.

        ⚠ FIX 2026-08-10 (code review, CRITICAL) - EVERY PICKLIST ON THE OBJECT IS ENUMERATED
        BELOW - Salutation, GeocodeAccuracy, LeadSource, Status, Industry, Rating,
        Asset_Type__c, Broker_Priority__c, Deal_Type__c, Disqualification_Reason__c,
        Parse_Confidence__c and Sale_Process__c - because a record type file that omits a
        picklist silently drops ALL of that picklist's values from that type. This is the same
        rule already enforced under NDA__c, Contract_Review__c, Disposition__c and LOI__c; the
        original brief that produced this file omitted it, which was a plan defect, not a
        deliberate narrowing. The full value set was pulled from a LIVE ORG DESCRIBE
        (`sf sobject describe, sobject=Lead`), never from this repo's field files, because
        `sf project retrieve` unions local and remote picklist values and a repo field file can
        list values the org no longer has. Both Lead record types (this one and IR_Investor)
        carry the IDENTICAL, COMPLETE value set with the SAME single default (Status = New) -
        narrowing either record type's values is a separate, deliberate change for later, and
        this fix is deliberately behaviour-neutral for every existing Lead.

        ⚠ FIX ROUND 2 (2026-08-10, check-only dry-run against usman-dpeg, deploy id
        0Afiw000000HR8fCAG) - added <businessProcess>Lead_Process</businessProcess>. Lead,
        Opportunity, Case and Solution record types each REQUIRE a BusinessProcess - a platform
        rule, confirmed by "Required field is missing: businessProcess" on this record type in
        the dry-run output. See objects/Lead/businessProcesses/Lead_Process.businessProcess-meta.xml
        for the full rationale, including why Status remains enumerated in BOTH places (this
        RecordType's own <picklistValues> AND the referenced BusinessProcess), and why one shared
        process serves both Lead record types rather than one each.

        ⚠ FIX ROUND 3 (2026-08-10, iterative check-only dry-runs against usman-dpeg, root-caused
        by bisection on Contact/recordTypes/Broker.recordType-meta.xml) - Salutation AND
        GeocodeAccuracy REMOVED. Both failed with a GENERIC "An unexpected error occurred...
        ErrorId..." rather than a clean field-level message. Root cause, confirmed by bisection
        on the Contact sibling case (identical shape, cheaper to isolate there): both are
        COMPOUND-FIELD SUB-COMPONENTS - `compoundFieldName` in the live describe reads `Name`
        for Salutation and `Address` for GeocodeAccuracy - and every field that failed had this
        set while every field that passed did not. RecordType.picklistValues cannot restrict a
        compound field's sub-picklist; the platform does not report this as a clean validation
        error, it throws generically instead. THE RULE FOR ALL FUTURE TASKS IN THIS PLAN: a
        picklist that is a compound-field sub-component must NEVER be enumerated in a
        RecordType's picklistValues - check `compoundFieldName` in the describe, not just
        `type`, before adding any field to this file. The remaining TEN picklists below
        (LeadSource, Status, Industry, Rating, Asset_Type__c, Broker_Priority__c, Deal_Type__c,
        Disqualification_Reason__c, Parse_Confidence__c, Sale_Process__c) were each dry-run-
        validated and remain enumerated identically on both Lead record types.
        [SUPERSEDED 2026-08-14: the count is now ELEVEN - see FIX ROUND 4 below. Everything
        else in this paragraph still stands.]

        FIX ROUND 4 (2026-08-14, Phase 1 re-review finding C4, CRITICAL) - Listing_Status__c
        ADDED. It is the eleventh picklist and was the ONLY custom picklist on Lead missing
        from this file; its two on-page neighbours, Sale_Process__c and Parse_Confidence__c,
        were both already here. The field is created by P1-A of the acquisition-observations
        pack, and the brief that created it did not carry the FIX 2026-08-10 rule forward - the
        same class of plan defect that produced that fix in the first place.

        WHY THIS IS CRITICAL RATHER THAN TIDYING. Under the drop-on-omission rule asserted
        above, an omitted picklist renders on this record type with ZERO selectable values.
        Deal_Facts_Required_For_Under_Review blocks Status = 'Under Review' while
        Listing_Status__c is blank, so an analyst would see a rendered, editable, FLS-granted
        field they cannot satisfy, and every pipeline-created Lead would strand at 'New'. That
        is byte-identical to the outage the Lead_Record_Page fix (review C1) exists to prevent,
        reached by a third route. Nothing catches it: no Apex writes Lead.Status = 'Under
        Review', the validation rule reads its fields in system context so it fires regardless,
        and a picklist with no available values still accepts a system-mode Apex write.

        THE DROP-ON-OMISSION RULE IS REPO-ASSERTED, NOT MEASURED IN THIS ORG. It is stated in
        the FIX 2026-08-10 paragraph above, in requirements section 3 and in the P1-D4 README,
        and this file already acts on it - but no describe in this org has confirmed it. This
        edit was made anyway because it is FREE if the assertion is wrong (enumerating the
        complete value set is a no-op) and pipeline-stopping if it is right. Phase 3 hits the
        same question for the LOI and PSA record types, where the answer CHANGES what must ship
        rather than being free insurance. The cheap falsification is written out in
        agent-output/runbook-acquisition-observations-phase1.md, section 1, under the heading
        "C4 (re-review, 2026-08-14)".

        SHAPE. Both values, no default, matching the field's own valueSetDefinition order
        (On Market, Off Market; sorted=false) and matching the Sale_Process__c block in this
        same file. The field has no <default>, deliberately - the LLM is instructed not to
        guess it - so neither value carries one here either. Listing_Status__c is a plain
        custom picklist with no compoundFieldName, so FIX ROUND 3's prohibition does not apply.

        DEPLOY ORDER. This file must not be deployed before P1-A, which creates
        Lead.Listing_Status__c; a picklistValues block naming a field that does not exist yet
        fails the deploy. P1-A already precedes P1-D1, which is where this file ships, so the
        existing sequence is correct - do not reorder it.
    -->
